WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.543
How many motor neurons does a chick embryo's leg column hold at the peak?

2
00:00:08.403 --> 00:00:09.943
And how many are left afterwards?

3
00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:16.233
At the peak, the column that serves its leg holds just over 20,000 motor neurons.

4
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:21.003
These are Viktor Hamburger's raw counts, from 1975.

5
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:26.093
By about day 10 of incubation, roughly 12,000 are left.

6
00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:31.273
Nothing is wrong with this embryo.

7
00:00:32.273 --> 00:00:35.433
It has both legs. It will hatch, and it will walk.

8
00:00:38.217 --> 00:00:39.397
So, two questions.

9
00:00:40.297 --> 00:00:44.677
How could anyone tell those neurons were made and then died, rather than never made?

10
00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:47.367
And what decides how many stay?

11
00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:50.447
This lesson carries one idea.

12
00:00:51.327 --> 00:00:54.187
Make too many, then let the target keep what it can support.

13
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:01.840
A target is the tissue an axon grows to and connects with.

14
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.300
Here, it is the muscles of the leg.

15
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:09.710
In the chick limb experiments, changing the target changed how many of the neurons

16
00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:10.710
survived.

17
00:01:11.283 --> 00:01:14.023
It did not detectably change how many were made.

18
00:01:15.017 --> 00:01:17.837
More target means fewer deaths, up to a ceiling.

19
00:01:17.837 --> 00:01:21.357
No target can rescue a neuron that is not there.

20
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:25.547
Some neurons die on a schedule that the target does not set.

21
00:01:26.347 --> 00:01:27.607
The lesson ends on them.

22
00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:33.330
Takeaway: the embryo makes too many, and in the limb, the target helps set how many

23
00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:33.750
survive.
